We are a non-judgemental lot, by and large, Mysore style ashtangis
If you want to change it up, use props, make it instagrammable, do poses from second or third mixed into primary, nobody is going to stop you and nobody is going to go on record saying you shouldn't

If you haven't read 'how ashtanga causes meditation' here it is for ease of access:

  Asana,Drsti,Bandha,Ujjayi - four concurrent physical body control tasks
The mind has four slots for this kind of task
Doing these four tasks fills the mind, which makes it 'stop'
This is known as the attention saturation explanation

So if your practice involves a lot of management, decision making, exercising of rights and of free will, then that is fine, and I salute your health and happiness
Just be aware that we choose how much to engage with the full four-parts. Some choose to go all-out, some never do, and most everyone else is somewhere in between.

So is the question do we want props? Or is the question do we want slower or lighter meditation?

Does Iyengar style yoga cause saturation through overcomplication?
